{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Neighbourhood policing, strong action on antisocial behaviour, compulsory drug testing/treatment, tougher gun/knife controls, and ID cards to combat fraud/illegal working; immigration points system favouring skills, English requirement for settlement, biometric visas and employer penalties; pro‑EU stance (lead reform, possible euro if tests met), internationalism (UN/G8 leadership, 0.7% aid), and creation of a Single Equality Act and Equality & Human Rights Commission. TEXT_F: Law‑and‑order focus with 5,000 extra police per year, elected police commissioners, 20,000 extra prison places, end early release, tougher sentencing, Homeland Security Minister, and reclassifying cannabis to Class B; restrictive immigration and border control (24/7 port surveillance, restored embarkation controls, British Border Control Police, Australian‑style points system, process asylum claims outside the UK, fixed annual refugee quota, overall immigration limit set by Parliament, repatriate asylum powers from the EU); strong national sovereignty over supranational authority (oppose EU Constitution, no Euro, restore Social Chapter opt‑out, repatriate fisheries control, English votes for English laws); defense of traditional practices (free vote to overturn hunting ban) and tougher action on illegal traveller camps.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_F consistently argues from TAN commitments—prioritising national identity, sovereignty, border control, and punitive law‑and‑order—rather than merely discussing these topics administratively. TEXT_E mixes some security‑oriented measures with technocratic immigration management and notably GAL‑leaning equality and internationalist commitments, making it less aligned with a strongly TAN electorate profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
